	<font face=arial size=-1>
<font size=+1><b>UWO:O At E3</b></font><p>
E3 has come and gone and what we are left with is a plethora of previews, first looks and general opinions about the latest batch of games to be released in the not too distant future.  One of these games, and the reason you are here reading this, is the upcoming offering from the creators of Ultima Online entitled Ultima Worlds Online: Origin.  What follows below is a summary of the press that has come out of E3 or come as a result of E3's showing.  If there are any articles I have missed, please feel free to let me know about it by emailing me at <a href=gasper@vaultnetwork.com>gasper@vaultnetwork.com</a>.
<p>
The first tier of groups reporting on Origin are those who have a direct interest in the game and actually have sites dedicated to the title or are involved directly in the rpg genre.  First and foremost, the <a href=http://rpgvault.ign.com/ target=_blank class=post>RPG Vault</a>, our parent site, has a <a href=http://rpgvault.ign.com/features/specials/uo2E3.shtml target=_blank class=post>report</a> about what Origin is and what they saw behind closed doors at E3.  As a concluding remark, Richard Aihoshi, aka Jonric, had this to say:
<blockquote>Combining an interesting variation on a familiar setting, a number of features designed to make it more accessible and more easily playable, and some eye-catching graphics, Ultima Worlds Online: Origin carries great promise for the not too distant future of the persistent online world genre. Add in the presence of an experienced and accomplished development team, season with Lead Designer Damion "Ubiq" Schubert's assurance that the game is in very good hands, and this work in progress is one that we will continue to watch with even greater anticipation over the coming months.</blockquote>
You can also take a look at <a href=http://rpgvault.ign.com/features/media/e3uo2.shtml target=_blank class=post>their E3 media gallery with screenshots and concept art from Origin</a>.  <a href=http://www.stratics.com target=_blank class=post>Stratics</a> also had a representative covering Origin at E3 in the form of Rainman.  His coverage, consisting of not only Origin but of his entire time at E3, may be found <a href=http://uo2.stratics.com/staff/Rainman/e3/e3_coverage.shtml target=_blank class=post>here</a>.  Rainman had this to say about the look of the game:
<blockquote>The detail of the textures was very impressive, as were the ambient sounds. When you walked closer too an animal, their noise slowly became louder, just as in real life. When you walked away, the sound faded into the background. Blackthorn's character looked absolutely STUNNING in-game. He literally looked as close to the RL actual action figure as I've EVER seen a 3D character look. The player characters are not so perfect looking, obviously, as they sacrifice that realism for massive amounts of customization in terms of clothing, armor, etc..</blockquote>
<a href=http://www.rpgplanet.com target=_blank class=post>RPG Planet</a> throws their hat in the ring with a <a href=http://www.rpgplanet.com/content/firstlooks/uo2/ target=_blank class=post>first look at Origin</a> and rouding out the first tier are <a href=http://lum.xrgaming.net target=_blank class=post>Lum's</a> and <a href=http://www.markeedragon.com target=_blank class=post>Markee Dragon</a>.  Both of these guys went above and beyond the call of duty, not only bringing us details on what they saw but also providing a transcribed log of the presentation and in Lum's case, a discussion that took place afterwards.  Lum starts with <a href=http://lum.xrgaming.net/e3/origin.html target=_blank class=post>his own opinions of what he saw</a> and then, before getting into the presentation, he concludes with this remark:
<blockquote>So what's my call? Too early to tell. The rapid dumping of core features (housing, guilds, rideable mounts) could mean a ship-by-this-date-or-else mentality that destroys every other Origin product, or it could mean a new commitment to quality and not promising what you're not sure you can deliver. Depends on who you talk to. At any event, the game is the farthest from release of any of the upcoming MMORPGs I saw at E3 (Shadowbane has a similar release date, but seemed much further along in development). We probably will see another E3 before release, at which time we can deliver a more reasoned judgement.</blockquote>
Be sure to <a href=http://lum.xrgaming.net/e3/origin.html target=_blank class=post>check it out</a>.  <a href=http://www.markeedragon.com/e3.htm target=_blank class=post>Markee Dragon's E3 coverage</a> is another recap of the day's events but it's important to note that the presentation and following QA session are different from Lum's.  Just because you've read one log of the presentation doesn't mean you've read them all.  In their final comments, this is said of the movement of the characters:
<blockquote>Let me tell you, the motion capture for the player and NPC animations is unbelievable. We spent a few days looking at character movement in many games throughout the E3 show and most of the others looked like plastic people with a stick up their butts and the coordination of a drunk. UO2 character movement is the most dynamic and exciting we've seen in a very long time. The unique combat moves are better than any we've seen in the past and are as fluid and graceful as a dancer.</blockquote>
We're now done with the first group of sites, those very intimately involved with the game itself.  Now on to the larger, more general gaming sites to see what they have to offer!  First up, Trent Ward of <a href=http://pc.ign.com target=_blank class=post>PC IGN</a> wrote a <a href=http://pc.ign.com/previews/14803.html target=_blank class=post>preview of Origin</a> based on his experience at E3 and the showing that took place there.  He sums up how many of us feel in his intro paragraph:
<blockquote>One of the most incredible presentations at this year's E3 show was Starr Long's back room unveiling of the next world in the Ultima Online series. Now titled Ultima Worlds Online: Origin (a tip of the hat to the now fully assimilated Origin Systems), this new massively multiplayer RPG promises to finally deliver the realistic gaming world that we've all been anticipating for so long. What Starr and the rest of the Origin team are doing is to put together the gameplay, graphics and animation that we would demand out of a single player game together with the vast worlds and heavily populated environments that we've gotten used to in our online games. If the hybrid works, it could be the game that will finally bring the rest of the gaming world into the online fold. More importantly, the game's title alone suggests that this may be just the first of a long series of vastly different online worlds. 
</blockquote>
<a href=http://www.dailyradar.com target=_blank class=post>Daily Radar</a> also got up an early preview of Origin.  They commented that, "From the possible hundred plus character animations to the amazing graphics to the possibility of a 3D Ultima, we are excited."  <a href=http://www.gamespy.com target=_blank class=post>Gamespy</a> comes next with their <a href=http://www.gamespy.com/e3/uo_a.shtm target=_blank class=post>E3 report</a> written by Tina "Cassandra" Haumersen.  A hard sell indeed, this is how she sums it up:
<blockquote>Well, you may ask, does the game look like it will be any fun to play? If you can remove the psuedo-references to the wonderful world of Britannia that we all know and love and judge it merely on its merits as a massively multiplayer online game, then the answer is yes. It does look like it will be a game people can enjoy, even though the graphics that we were shown weren't really all that much better than what is currently available in AC or EQ. As an Ultima game, however, I'm very disappointed. Perhaps I shouldn't be though. Its as much related to the Ultima universe as UO is, which is to say that its not in the least other than superficially. I think the game may do well upon release, although I know that bigger and better things are coming from other companies in the MMORPG genre. 
</blockquote>
Included is also the post-presentation conversation also to be found on <a href=http://lum.xrgaming.net/ target=_blank class=post>Lum's</a>.  Heading off in another direction to a site relatively new to the gaming realm (but certainly not to the hardware buffs), <a href=http://www.sharkyextreme.com target=_blank class=post>Sharky Extreme</a> throws up their <a href=http://www.sharkyextreme.com/games/glimpses/ea/origin/ target=_blank class=post>extremely brief First Glimpse at Origin</a>.  Turning another corner, we hit the good old <a href=http://www.avault.com target=_blank class=post>Adrenaline Vault</a>.  While not a preview or first look or anything of the such per se...it is <a href=http://www.avault.com/articles/getarticle.asp?name=sequels target=_blank class=post>a look at the upcoming sequels</a> we are all anticipating.  Included is of course what once was and could probably still loosely be considered the sequel to UO.  On <a href=http://www.avault.com/articles/getarticle.asp?name=sequels&page=5 target=_blank class=post>page 5</a>, they sum things up by saying:<blockquote>All-in-all, Origin seems to be modernizing all of its elements which, once innovative, are now lagging behind the emerging circle of competitors. Between the use of top-notch video capabilities, the complete renovation of the Britannian world and the series' emergence into 3D, Ultima Online Worlds certainly possesses the ambition and design team to shake up a somewhat slumbering genre.</blockquote>
Across the Atlantic, we get a little <a href=http://www.gamespot.co.uk/news/2000/05/6795.html target=_blank class=post>First Look from GameSpot UK</a> while a little closer to home (for some of us), <a href=http://www.cdmag.com target=_blank class=post>Computer Games Online</a> brings us a nice little <a href=http://www.cdmag.com/Home/home.html?article=/articles/028/002/uo2_preview.html target=_blank class=post>Origin preview from E3</a> by Cindy Yans.  They run through the gamut of issues, introduce us to the team and generally provide a great, fact filled preview of what will surely be a bit hit next year.  She's sums it up by saying, "While predecessors such as Meridian and The Realm gave us the flavor of the concept, only the creators of Ultima managed to pull off this type of game in a HUGE way. And now, the second generation of this genre is about to explode with, of course, Origin still at the helm."
<p>
Another <a href=http://www.gamerevolution.com/previews/featured/uo2.htm target=_blank class=post>short little preview</a> comes to us from <a href=http://www.gamerevolution.com target=_blank class=post>Game Revolution</a> and last but certainly not least, <a href=http://www.elecplay.com target=_blank class=post>Electric Playground</a> brings us this <a href=http://www.elecplay.com/features/E3/e3-online.html target=_blank class=post>feature from E3</a> by Sean Miller.  Sean packs so much into a single page preview that it's hard to find just one thing to quote.  I finally settled on the following which should come as a welcome addition for any player who yearns for a real storyline in these online worlds:
<blockquote>The most ambitious and interesting plans for Origin involve the world's story line. The developers plan on having a dynamic story written and mapped out for the game's first eighteen months and will hire actors to play key characters within these stories. One of the problems with many of today's persistent worlds is a lack of overall direction or feeling of story within the games. Origin seems to have realized this and moved towards finding a solution and increasing the depth of their new world. 
</blockquote>
It's time to end this summary of the press coming out of E3 concerning Origin but before we go, we should tip our hats to the team behind the game.  <a href=http://www.arstechnica.com target=_blank class=post>Ars Technica</a> gave Origin <a href=http://www.arstechnica.com/etc/games/gars-05202000-1.html target=_blank class=post>their award for Massively-Multiplyer RPG game of show</a>.  Congratulations to the team and everyone involved in making Origin.
<p>
-Gasper

</font>

